Issues.Body:
### What happens

`@tailwindcss/node`'s URL rebase has no notion of Windows drive letters. When the entry stylesheet and an imported stylesheet are on different drives, `rebaseUrl` joins the imported file's base with the relative URL and then asks `path.posix.relative` for a path from the entry's root; the drive letter (`H:`) is treated as an ordinary path segment, so the emitted URL climbs out of the entry's drive:

```css
background-image: url("../../../../../../../H:/…/app/images/probe.svg");
```

The same stylesheet on the same drive emits the correct relative URL:

```css
background-image: url("./app/images/probe.svg");
```

### Versions

- `@tailwindcss/node` 4.3.3 (Tailwind CSS 4.3.3), run under Bun 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3010e5553438c60f6bd76d981976867c`)
- Windows 11 10.0.26200 x64

### Minimal repro (no Bun plugin)

Entry stylesheet under `C:/Users/…/AppData/Local/Temp/ulak-tw-crossdrive/entry.css`:

```css
@import "H:/…/tw-repro/app/app.css";
.card2{color:red}
```

Imported stylesheet `H:/…/tw-repro/app/app.css`:

```css
.card{background-image:url("./images/probe.svg")}
```

with `H:/…/tw-repro/app/images/probe.svg` beside it.

```ts
import { compile } from "@tailwindcss/node";

const css = `@import "H:/…/tw-repro/app/app.css";\n.card2{color:red}\n`;
const compiler = await compile(css, {
  base: "C:/Users/…/AppData/Local/Temp/ulak-tw-crossdrive",
  onDependency() {},
  shouldRewriteUrls: true,
});
console.log(compiler.build([]));
```

Actual cross-drive output:

```css
.card {
  background-image: url("../../../../../../../H:/…/tw-repro/app/images/probe.svg");
}
.card2 {
  color: red;
}
```

Same inputs with the imported stylesheet copied under the C: entry directory:

```css
.card {
  background-image: url("./app/images/probe.svg");
}
```

The repro was run both ways from one script; the outputs above are the actual runs.

### The code

`packages/@tailwindcss-node/src/urls.ts` at `9b2c925c19aae46ff4f9cf54a2fa6d2dbac7fa19`, lines 99-113:

```ts
function rebaseUrl(url: string, base: string, root: string) {
  if (url[0] === '/') return url

  let absoluteUrl = path.posix.join(normalizePath(base), url)
  let relativeUrl = path.posix.relative(normalizePath(root), absoluteUrl)

  // If the path points to a file in the same directory, `path.relative` will
  // remove the leading `./` and we need to add it back in order to still
  // consider the path relative
  if (!relativeUrl.startsWith('.')) {
    relativeUrl = './' + relativeUrl
  }

  return relativeUrl
}
```

Permalink: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/blob/9b2c925c19aae46ff4f9cf54a2fa6d2dbac7fa19/packages/%40tailwindcss-node/src/urls.ts#L99-L113

`compile()` calls it for every loaded stylesheet when `shouldRewriteUrls` is set (`packages/@tailwindcss-node/src/compile.ts`, lines 53-60: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/blob/9b2c925c19aae46ff4f9cf54a2fa6d2dbac7fa19/packages/%40tailwindcss-node/src/compile.ts#L53-L60). `@tailwindcss/vite` passes `shouldRewriteUrls: true` at `packages/@tailwindcss-vite/src/index.ts:405`, so it goes through the same `rewriteUrls` path (https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/blob/9b2c925c19aae46ff4f9cf54a2fa6d2dbac7fa19/packages/%40tailwindcss-vite/src/index.ts#L402-L405).

### Why this surfaced through Bun

The published `bun-plugin-tailwind` 0.1.2 bundles the same rebase code inline: its `index.mjs` contains the `path.posix.join` + `path.posix.relative` pair, and it declares no `@tailwindcss/node` dependency. Our original report was oven-sh/bun#48385 (a `bun-plugin-tailwind` Windows build failing to resolve the drive-climbing URL); the first filing on this tracker, #20681, was closed because the Bun plugin is not part of this repository. The failing path, however, is the `@tailwindcss/node` code above, which this repository does maintain.

### Expected

A cross-drive import should produce a resolvable URL — either the absolute URL it already holds, or a path that keeps the drive (for example `H:/…/app/images/probe.svg`), never a relative path that climbs out of the entry's drive.

### Possible fix direction (not tested)

Use a drive-aware relative computation on Windows (`path.win32.relative` when both sides are drive-qualified, then convert to URL form), or skip rebasing and keep the absolute URL when `path.parse(root).root !== path.parse(base).root`. Either way, `path.posix.relative` alone cannot express a path between two drives.

## Summary

#17647 made `shadow-inherit`, `inset-shadow-inherit`, `text-shadow-inherit` and `drop-shadow-inherit` set the color to `inherit`, and the first three return nothing when they get an opacity modifier, because `inherit` can't go inside `color-mix()`. `drop-shadow-inherit/50` still generates it:

```css
.drop-shadow-inherit\/50 {
  --tw-drop-shadow-color: color-mix(in srgb, inherit 50%, transparent);
  @supports (color: color-mix(in lab, red, red)) {
    --tw-drop-shadow-color: color-mix(in oklab, color-mix(in oklab, inherit 50%, transparent) var(--tw-drop-shadow-alpha), transparent);
  }
  --tw-drop-shadow: var(--tw-drop-shadow-size);
}
```

the drop-shadow color branch does have an `inherit` case, but it checks the value returned by `resolveThemeColor`, which has already applied the modifier. so with `/50` the value is `color-mix(in oklab, inherit 50%, transparent)` and the check never matches.

the fix returns early for `inherit` with a modifier before resolving the color, the same `if (candidate.modifier) return` the other three utilities use. `drop-shadow-inherit` without a modifier is unchanged.

## Test plan

- added `drop-shadow-inherit/50` and `drop-shadow-inherit/[0.5]` to the list of drop-shadow candidates that must generate nothing in `utilities.test.ts`. on `main` the test fails with the css above; with the fix it passes
- `vitest run src/utilities.test.ts src/intellisense.test.ts` in `packages/tailwindcss`: 421 passed
- `prettier --check` on the changed files and `pnpm changelog check` pass

**What version of Tailwind CSS are you using?**

v4.3.4 (`tailwindcss` + `@tailwindcss/node`), with lightningcss 1.33.0.

**What build tool (or framework if it abstracts the build tool) are you using?**

Reproduced with `@tailwindcss/node` `compile` + `optimize` directly. It also affects the Vite plugin, since that uses the same optimize step.

**What browser are you using?**

N/A. The rule is missing from the built CSS.

**Reproduction**

```js
import { compile, optimize } from '@tailwindcss/node';

const input = `@tailwind utilities;
@utility join {
  display: inline-flex;
  @scope (&) {
    > .item { z-index: 2; }
  }
}`;
const c = await compile(input, { base: process.cwd(), onDependency() {} });
const raw = c.build(['join']);
console.log(raw);
console.log(optimize(raw, { minify: false }).code);
```

Compiled output: `@scope (&)` is hoisted out of `.join`, but the relative selector is kept as is.

```css
.join { display: inline-flex; }
@scope (:is(.join)) {
  > .item { z-index: 2; }
}
```

The optimize step then warns and drops the rule:

```
Found 1 warning while optimizing generated CSS:
│ @scope (:is(.join)) {
│   > .item {
┆   ^-- Invalid empty selector
```

```css
.join { display: inline-flex; }
@scope (.join) {
}
```

**Describe your issue**

A relative selector (one that starts with a combinator) inside a nested `@scope (&)` is valid CSS. When Tailwind hoists the `@scope` to the top level, it emits that selector unchanged, and Lightning CSS can't parse a relative selector directly under a top-level `@scope`. It parses fine while the `@scope` is still nested in a style rule. The declarations are silently lost; the only signal is a build warning.

Real-world impact: daisyUI 5's `join` component loses its focus/hover `z-index` rules in every Tailwind v4 build (saadeghi/daisyui#4851).

**Suggested fix:** when hoisting `@scope (&)`, prefix leading-combinator selectors with `:scope`. For example, `> .item` becomes `:scope > .item`. That is semantically identical per CSS Cascade 6 and parses in Lightning CSS. Writing `:scope > .item` by hand in the source works today.

The underlying parser gap is in Lightning CSS (relative selectors are valid directly inside `@scope` per spec). We couldn't open an issue there, because issue creation appears to be restricted to collaborators.

## Summary

Fixes #20657.

The `color-mix(…)` polyfill switches the color space of the fallback to `srgb`. It reads the color space from the third node of the function, as in `color-mix(in oklab, …)`. But the color interpolation method is optional, and with a single color there is no third node:

```html
<div class="bg-[color-mix(var(--color-red-500))]"></div>
```

Then `colorspace.kind` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'kind')`, and the whole build fails. Because the compiler keeps the candidate, the next builds fail as well, e.g. in `--watch` mode after the class is removed again.

This PR only switches the color space when the third node exists. The fallback is then created like for any other `color-mix(…)` with theme variables:

```css
.bg-\[color-mix\(var\(--color-red-500\)\)\] {
  background-color: color-mix(oklch(63.7% .237 25.331));
}

@supports (color: color-mix(in lab, red, red)) {
  .bg-\[color-mix\(var\(--color-red-500\)\)\] {
    background-color: color-mix(var(--color-red-500));
  }
}
```

The other `nodes[2]` reads (`canonicalize-calc-expressions.ts`, `constant-fold-declaration.ts` and `canonicalize-candidates.ts`) already check `nodes.length === 5` first.

## Test plan

- Added a test to the `color-mix(…)` polyfill tests in `packages/tailwindcss/src/index.test.ts`. It fails without the change (`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'kind')`) and passes with it.
- `pnpm exec vitest run --hideSkippedTests` at the root: 5518 passed, 1 skipped, 1 failed. The failure also happens on `main`: `variants with the same root are sorted deterministically` times out after 5s on my machine. 7 test files in `@tailwindcss/postcss` and `@tailwindcss/upgrade` don't load, because I can't build `@tailwindcss/oxide` here (no Rust toolchain). Same on `main`.
- `pnpm changelog check` passes, and `prettier --check` passes on the changed files.
- Reproduced the crash with `@tailwindcss/cli@4.3.3` and on Tailwind Play.
